      "text": "To evaluate \\(\\log_3 (27 \\sqrt{3})\\), we will first express the argument of the logarithm in terms of a base that is easier to handle. We know that \\(27 = 3^3\\) and \\(\\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 (27 \\sqrt{3}) = \\log_3 (3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2})\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the property of exponents that states \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\). Applying this property, we get:\n\n\\[\n3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{6/2 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^{7/2})\n\\]\n\nBy the definition of logarithms, \\(\\log_3 (3^{7/2}) = \\frac{7}{2}\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
