      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\), we start by expressing the argument of the logarithm as a power of 3. We know that \\(27\\) and \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) can be written in terms of powers of 3:\n\n\\[27 = 3^3\\]\n\\[\\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}\\]\n\nThus, we can rewrite the expression \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\) as:\n\n\\[\\log_3 (3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2})\\]\n\nUsing the properties of exponents, specifically \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\), we can combine the exponents:\n\n\\[3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{6/2 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\\log_3 (3^{7/2})\\]\n\nBy the definition of logarithms, \\(\\log_b (b^x) = x\\), we have:\n\n\\[\\log_3 (3^{7/2}) = \\frac{7}{2}\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\\).",
